INT. FERRY TICKET BOOTH - NIGHT

A glass box at the end of a pier. A single brass lamp. Rain slides down the windows. Outside, the last ferry's lights shrink into fog.

ODETTE ARMSTRONG, 60s, uniform cap, spectacles on a chain, sits at a worn desk. Before her: a cassette recorder, the kind with piano keys. A thermos.

She presses PLAY.

ODETTE (V.O.)
(recorded, younger, warm)
Testing. Testing. This is Odette Armstrong, ticket agent, Pier Nine. If you're hearing this, I've either retired or I've lost my mind.

She smiles. Stops the tape. Rewinds a little. Plays again.

ODETTE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded)
Thirty years on this pier. I want to record what I remember. The first fare I ever sold was a dollar twenty.

She pours tea. Her hand trembles slightly.

ODETTE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded)
The worst winter was '88. The ice stopped everything. I slept in this booth for three nights. I was happier than I'd ever been.

She stops the tape.

ODETTE
(to herself)
Happier.

She looks at the empty pier. At the dark water.

She presses PLAY.

ODETTE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded)
If I could tell the person listening one thing, it would be this. Don't wait for the boat. The boat always leaves.

The thermos lid falls. Rolls across the floor. She doesn't pick it up.

ODETTE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded)
Go to the pier before the boat. Go to the sea before the pier.

Her jaw tightens. She stabs the STOP key. The click echoes.

She stands. Pulls the uniform cap from her head. Looks at it.

ODETTE
Thirty years.

She hurls the cap against the glass. It bounces off. Falls.

She tears the cassette from the machine, raises it to smash it, and stops. Her chest heaves.

Slowly, she slides it into her breast pocket. Picks up the cap. Sets it on the desk.

She switches off the lamp. Dark. Rain.

ODETTE (CONT'D)
Go to the sea.

She pushes open the door. Fog pours in. She steps out onto the pier.
